tmux — cheatsheet

The half of tmux that is sys's: the three buttons, which run sh/p, the
two options that make them work, the layout every pane lands in, the
pointer that follows it, and a selection that is X's too. The prefix and
the status line are your own tmux.conf. Nothing here is unbound, so every
tmux default still works underneath.

Only what tmux can see is here. p's input is a word on the screen, which
no command can reach; the directory you are in is not, so opening it is
o at the prompt and needs no key at all.

the buttons — acme's three, one meaning each in every pane (design/mouse)
  left      this: the word under the pointer becomes cur, and nothing
            runs (p -c); in a frame, ed and fm go there
  right     look: open what the word names (p); in a frame, ed or fm
            shows it and takes the eye; a double tap, the touchpad's
            right click, does the same
  middle    do: hand the word to the shell, at its prompt (p -a)
  drag      select, then say the selection as a left click says a word

the keys — ` is your prefix; press it twice to type one
  `p `a     the last word said, again: open it, or hand it to the shell
  p a       in copy mode: the same for the cursor's word, or for the
            selection when there is one
  ` Enter   this pane to master, swapped with the one there

In a frame the border over a slot of tabs is a button too, and alt-t
hides the terminals: studio/cheatsheet.

A pane whose program asks for the mouse, ed, fm and the lens among them,
takes the buttons itself and means the same by them: ed hands its word,
or its selection when the click falls in it, to p, and fm hears a right
click as enter. A drag in any other pane selects for X too, its primary
selection, and a copy in copy mode is X's clipboard: both through xclip,
for st takes no OSC 52 from tmux as it is built.

Nothing here names fm, so changing sh/o changes what a right click and
`p open, as it changes o. fm's keys are in fm/cheatsheet.

p asks the file system, then git, and never lets screen text become a
command — the word travels in a pane option, not on a command line.

  file:line     ed at that line
  a path        o, which dispatches on the extension; o never runs one
  a url         browser
  a hash        git show
  none of it    nothing

p -c stops earlier than any of it: the word is resolved, said, and left
in cur, and the verb is whatever you type next — o, e, git add `{c}. A
left click is also what raises a pane, so every click says this; that is
the price of select being a click, and m is the refund.

p -a stops before that table. One word goes as the path it resolved —
absolute, so it survives the trip — and several, a selection, as they
are. It lands at the prompt of the marked pane (select-pane -m), else
the frame's shell, which takes the eye, else the last pane; one found so
takes it only while a shell waits at its prompt. It goes in as a paste,
and no Enter, so the chord is: type wc -l, middle-click the word, press
Return.

A path is one word only because word-separators is " ". With the
default set, the word under ed/2-motion/04-fwd.go:12 comes back as
`motion`, and the address is lost. That one line is the whole enabler.

focus-events is the second option, and it is fm's: a view that writes
cur has to know whether it is the pane being looked at, or a background
one takes the pronoun from the shell on its next redraw. tmux forwards
the terminal's focus in and out to the pane only with this on.

A path is relative to the directory that printed it, which is the
pane's own until someone cds — the reason p asks that pane, and the
reason fm opens there.

the layout — main-vertical, re-applied when a window's panes change
  The shell is master and everything else stacks beside it, at
  main-pane-width 50%. spawn asks for a pane and never says where one
  goes, so there is no side to get wrong and nothing to keep in sync.
  Your own splits land the same way, which is why ` % and ` " no
  longer pick a side; ` Enter makes this pane the master.
  Only the window whose panes changed is laid out again, a split, a
  kill or a pane ending in it, so a pane resized by hand stays as it
  is while other windows come and go. A studio frame keeps its own.
  The pointer follows the pane you land in, split or selected, so
  the next right-click is already there. It never moves when it is
  already in that pane, so a click is not dragged off the word.

not here
  b e k l m R Tab { }   yours: status, pane name, clear, floating
                        pane, mouse, reload, last window, swap
  everything else       tmux's own, none of it unbound. ` ? lists
                        them all, ` / <key> explains one

change it: tmux/conf — edit, then ` R.
